<img src="01.Driving_Tappen_Zee.jpg" />

<p id='Extract'>It takes about 6 hours to get to Luray, Virginia, from Darien, Connecticut,
    and we left early so we would be able to get there while the caverns were 
    still open&mdash;and, if not, we could tour them first thing in the
    morning, as long as we could find a nearby place to camp. Preferably,
    for free.</p>

<p>But first we had to get across the Hudson River, preferably without going
    through New York City again. Our best bet was the Tappan Zee Bridge,
    which was still in operation. The Tappan Zee Bridge, officially known 
    as the Governor Malcolm Wilson Tappan Zee Bridge, was a significant 
    cantilever bridge in New York State. It spanned the Hudson River at one 
    of its widest points, connecting South Nyack in Rockland County with 
    Tarrytown in Westchester County. The bridge was named after the Tappan 
    Native American tribe and the Dutch word <q>zee,</q> meaning <q>sea,</q> 
    referring to the wide expanse of the river it crossed.</p>

<img src="02.Tappan_Zee.jpg" />

<p>Construction of the original bridge began in March 1952, and it opened 
    to traffic on December 15, 1955. It was designed to handle a maximum of 100,000 
    vehicles per day but by the 2000s, it was carrying an average of 140,000 vehicles 
    daily. This heavy usage led to significant wear and tear over the years,
    which is why it had to be replaced. The new bridge, with a different name,
    opened in 2017 but people still called it the <q>Tappan Zee Bridge</q>.</p>

<img src="10.I-81.jpg" />

<p>Eventually we got to Virginia, and I-81. This highway was supposed to have been finished,
    and our map said it was; but in reality there were many sections still under
    construction. Still, you couldn't say the views from the road weren't
    spectacular!</p>

<p>But, despite all that, we managed to get to Luray before the caverns closed,
    mainly because the last tour was at six. (I had assumed a 5 PM closing time.)
    So we took it!</p>

<img src="20.Entrance.jpg" />

<img src="21.Slides.png" class="Right" />

<p>Now, of <i>course</i> I wanted to take photos. I had my camera, but hadn't realized
    it was on its last frame of film, and I'd left the new roll in the car. However, in 
    the 1960s, color slides were a popular souvenir item sold at tourist attractions, 
    including Luray Caverns. Visitors could purchase strips of slides as a way to 
    remember their experience without the need for a camera or the skills to capture 
    the beauty themselves. These slides featured professionally taken photographs of 
    the attraction's highlights, allowing tourists to bring home a piece of their 
    adventure in a format that could be easily mixed in with their own travel slides
    and viewed on a slide projector at home.</p>

<p>Which I did. To be honest, I can download far superior photos of Luray Caverns
    from the web. But for historical accuracy, here are the five color slides I
    purchased in the gift shop on our way out, for (as I recall) $1.25.</p>

<img src="22.Luray_Caverns.jpg" />
<div class="PhotoPanel">
    <div class="PhotoRow3">
        <img src="23.Luray_Caverns.jpg" />
        <img src="24.Luray_Caverns.jpg" />
        <img src="25.Luray_Caverns.jpg" />
