<img src="26.Luray_Caverns.jpg" />

<p>Now, across the road from the entrance to the caverns, was a bare dirt area
    on which people were camping. There weren't many RVs in those days, but a number of
    families had pitched tents on this area. And it was unmanaged, meaning there
    was no charge! So that was good. However, it was actually somewhat crowded.
    And I wasn't looking forward to trying to sleep on that rock-hard ground.</p>

<p>So here's where any experienced camper would start to see red flags. The bare
    area inclined down into a sort of gully, where there were bunches of thick,
    waving, green grass. And it just looked <i>so soft</i>. And that's where I 
    wanted to put the tent.</p>

<p>And Chris, with even less experience camping than I had, agreed.</p>

<img src="27.Camping_in_Gully.jpg" />

<p>So, on the soft grassy ground, we went to sleep beneath a sky of brilliant stars,
    and only one cloud on the horizon. We slept deeply, without a thought as to what 
    creates "gullies" and why would lush, tall grass grow at the bottom of one.</p>

<img src="28.Flooded.jpg" />

<p>The next thing either of us knew, we were engulfed by water, the tent was being
    dragged by a flash flood with us in it, entangled in our sleeping bags and
    the tent itself. Somehow we both got out without drowning. Somehow we managed
    to drag our sleeping bags and tent out of the flood. Luckily, most of our gear
    had been left in the car, parked up where all the smart people had camped.</p>

<p>I mean, if I had only <i>thought</i> about it. <i>Why</i> was the grass in
    that gully so green? When it rains, where else would the water go? And
    why hadn't I realized the rills and gullies in the packed ground above
    the result of erosion from probably frequent rains?</p>

<p>It was, according to the clock in my car, 4:10 AM. Obviously we weren't going
    to get any more sleep <i>this</i> night. And if we wanted to get any the next
    night, we were going to have to get this crap dried before then.</p>

<p>I remembered having seen a laundromat in the little town on our way to
    the caverns and we headed for that.</p>

<img src="30.Laundromat.jpg" />

<p>Luray being such a small town, I had figured we'd have to wait for the laundromat
    to open. I was hoping that might happen as soon as 7. But it was actually a
    24-hour laundry! So Chris and I gathered <i>all</i> our change and loaded
    our incredibly heavy, drenching wet sleeping bags, as well as the clothes we'd been
    wearing, into driers.</p>

<img src="31.Drying_Sleeping_Bags.jpg" />

<img src="32.Jukebox.png" class="Left" />

<p>Yeah, and no, I either didn't think of running the bags through a 
    washer to spin them drier, or didn't want to waste money on a whole
    wash cycle when all I wanted was the spin. (I'm OCD about such things.)
    So our stuff was all loaded into driers, and I had a quarter left over.
    And the laundromat had a jukebox!</p>

<p>Yes, a good old-fashioned Wurlitzer loaded up with the latest in 45s.
    And there was a new hit, a favorite, by the group The Archies.</p>

<img src="33.Sugar_Sugar.jpg" class="Right" />

<p>The Archies were a fictional band made up of characters from the 
    Archie Comics universe. They were created for a Saturday morning 
    cartoon show called <q>The Archie Show,</q> which aired in the late 
    1960s. The band consisted of Archie Andrews on guitar and lead vocals, 
    Reggie Mantle on bass, Jughead Jones on drums, Veronica Lodge on keyboard, 
    and Betty Cooper on tambourine and backup vocals. Even though 
    they were cartoon characters, The Archies had real hit songs, with 
    their most famous being <q>Sugar, Sugar</q>. It was a catchy tune that 
    became a number-one hit in real life, and many people loved to sing 
    and dance along to it.</p>

<p>So that's the one I spent my last quarter on.</p>

<p>And Chris and I listened and sang along while our sleeping bags dried.
    And the jukebox got to the end of the song.</p>

<p>And it started to play again.</p>

<p>And when the song finished, it started to play again!</p>

<p>Apparently the jukebox was broken. And, as the hour went by and the
    song continued to play over and over and over and <b>over</b>,
    it quickly went from being catchy to being grounds for murder.</p>

<p>And <i>then</i>, adding insult to injury, the dryer stopped&hellip;
    <i>And our sleeping bags were still wet!</i></p>

<p>So now, we had to wait until the bank across the street opened, so
    I could break a fiver and get change. (Yes, that's right; there
    was no change machine in the laundromat in 1969!) Luckily I didn't have
    to wait until 9 AM; a little restaurant next door opened at 7.
    So we refueled the dryers and napped in the car while we waited
    for the sleeping bags to be done, so we could actually start
    our next-to-the-last-day.</p>


